excessive absences or tardiness, reporting time inaccurately, or working
unauthorized overtime.
● Driving a Company vehicle at any time or your personal vehicle on Company
business while under the influence of alcohol or an illegal or controlled
substance (including marijuana).
● Violating any Company policy whether or not detailed in this
Handbook.
● Other circumstances that we determine warrants corrective
action.
f) Confidentiality
As a condition of employment, each employee must execute a Confidential Information
and Invention Assignment Agreement (“CIIAA”) prior to beginning employment with
Mainstay. Each employee is responsible for safeguarding confidential information
obtained in connection with their employment. In the course of your work, you may have
access to confidential information regarding Mainstay, its suppliers, its customers, or
perhaps even fellow employees. It is your responsibility to in no way reveal or divulge any
such information unless it is necessary for you to do so in the performance of your
duties. Access to confidential information should be on a "need-to-know" basis and must
be authorized by your manager. Any breach of this policy will not be tolerated and legal
action may be taken by Mainstay.
Nothing in this policy is intended to interfere with or discourage a good faith disclosure to
any governmental entity related to a suspected violation of the law. Employees cannot
and will not be held criminally or civilly liable under any applicable federal or state trade
secrets law for disclosing otherwise protected trade secrets and/or confidential or
proprietary information as long as the disclosure is made (i) to a federal, state, or local
government official, directly or indirectly, or to an attorney and solely for the purpose of
reporting or investigating a suspected violation of law; or (ii) in a complaint or other
document filed in a lawsuit or other proceeding, as long as such filing is made under seal.
In addition, nothing in this policy is intended to preclude or dissuade employees from
engaging in activities protected by state or federal law, including the National Labor
Relations Act, such as discussing wages, benefits or terms and conditions of
employment.
g) Conflict of Interest
You have an obligation to conduct business within guidelines that prohibit actual or
potential conflicts of interest. Business dealings with outside firms should not result in
unusual gain from those firms. Unusual gain refers to bribes, product bonuses, special
fringe benefits, unusual price breaks and other windfalls designed to ultimately benefit
you.
9
An actual or potential conflict of interest occurs when you are in a position to influence a
decision that may result in a personal gain for you or for a relative as a result of our
business dealing. No presumption of guilt is created by the mere existence of a
relationship with outside firms. However, if you have any influence on transactions
involving purchases, contracts or leases, it is imperative that you disclose the existence
of any actual or potential conflict of interest so that safeguards can be established to
protect all parties.
Where an employee is a “related party” of (a) a customer involved in an Mainstay
transaction, or (b) a supplier, vendor, or service provider to Mainstay, that employee
must immediately disclose his/her connection to the customer, supplier, vendor, or
service provider and, if requested by the Company, recuse him/herself from the
transaction and/or the customer, supplier, vendor, or service provider relationship
(including but not limited to all contract negotiations with a particular supplier, vendor, or
service provider). For purposes of this provision,
“related party” means a family member
(including family by marriage), and persons with whom the employee may have a
romantic or intimate relationship or another personal connection. If an employee is not
sure whether this provision applies to a particular situation, he or she should err on the
side of contacting the People Team for clarification.
i) Vendor Gifts
Employees are strictly prohibited from accepting gifts or entertainment from settlement
services companies (e.g., real estate brokers and agents, mortgage lenders and brokers,
title companies, escrow companies, home warranty companies, home and hazard
insurance companies, home and pest inspectors, real estate attorneys, appraisers, etc.),
regardless of value. Employees may accept gifts of nominal value (not exceeding
$250.00) from customers, suppliers, vendors, or service providers (e.g., data providers,
general contractors, recruiting firms, etc.) provided that such gifts are not excessive
and/or do not create any sort of obligation on any employee and/or the Company. Gifts
exceeding $250.00 require prior written approval from the Legal Department.
ii) Interpersonal Relationships
Familial, romantic, or intimate relationships involving a managerial employee and a
subordinate or other employee within the managerial employee’s sphere of influence
can create potential, perceived or actual conflicts of interest and may lead to possible
claims of harassment and discrimination. Consequently, such relationships are
discouraged and must be disclosed to the People Team. If there is any possibility that
you may be engaged in a behavior or enterprise that creates, or gives the appearance of
a conflict of interest, it is your responsibility to notify the People Team at
hr@mainstay.io.com.
10
iii) Outside Employment
While you render services to Mainstay, you agree that you will not engage in any other
employment, consulting or other business activity that interferes with the performance
of your job duties or otherwise creates a conflict of interests. Please note that your role
in Mainstay is your primary job and hence this will be your main priority. Outside
employment will not be considered an excuse for poor job performance or attendance,
which remain subject to disciplinary action up to and including termination of
employment. If outside work activity causes or contributes to job-related problems at
Mainstay, you may be asked to discontinue the outside employment.
Employees may not engage in any outside employment that competes or conflicts with
Mainstay or compromises Mainstay’s interests, goodwill or reputation. If it is determined
that your other employment creates a conflict, you must cease your employment with
Mainstay or leave your other employment.
If you are considering outside employment but are not sure if it complies with the rules
and guidelines in this policy, you should speak with your manager and email the People
Team at hr@mainstay.io to determine whether the outside employment complies with
this policy.
Employees may not use Mainstay’s property, facilities, equipment, supplies, IT systems
(such as computers, networks, email, telephones, or voicemail), trademarks, brand, or
reputation in connection with any outside employment. This prohibition also extends to
the unauthorized use or application of any confidential information and/or trade secrets
belonging to Mainstay. In addition, employees may not solicit or conduct any outside
business during their work time for Mainstay.
h) Background Checks
In accordance with applicable federal, state, and local laws, Mainstay may require
certain applicants and employees to satisfactorily complete a background check via
Checkr. Mainstay will consider job duties, among other factors, in determining what
constitutes satisfactory completion of the background check. All background checks
and information obtained from a background check, will be kept confidential and used
solely for employment purposes.
When a background check is required, you must complete Mainstays authorization form.
Failure to timely complete an authorization may result in termination of consideration of
your employment. Falsification or omission of information may result in denial of
employment or discipline, up to and including termination.
