i) Introductory Period
All new team members go through an introductory period of adjustment in order to learn
about Mainstay and about their job. During this time, the new team member will have
time to acclimate to the Company’s mission and values while also learning about their
new role’s expectations. Additionally, the introductory period gives the manager a
reasonable period of time to assess general working habits (punctuality, attendance,
working relationships, etc.), and to evaluate the employee’s ability to meet established
performance standards. During the introductory period, the team member will be given
the support and resources needed to learn and demonstrate the duties and skills
required in the new role and to achieve clearly articulated goals and expectations.
The introductory period for full-time employees is usually 90 days from the start of
employment unless state or local law provides otherwise. At its discretion, Mainstay may
extend the 90-day introductory period.
During this introductory period, your employment remains at-will, meaning either you or
the Company may decide to end the employment relationship at any time, with or
without cause. Completion of the introductory employment period does not alter an
employee’s at-will status, and employment may be terminated by the Company or the
employee during or after completion of the introductory period at any time, with or
without cause.
j) Employment Status
All employees are designated as either exempt or non-exempt under state and federal
wage and hour laws. The determination of whether an employee is exempt or
non-exempt is based on criteria established under the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA),
state, and local laws.
Exempt Employees
Also known as salaried employees, are exempt from the minimum wage and overtime
provisions of the FLSA, state, and local laws. Exempt employees are paid a fixed salary
for each week worked and salary deductions shall only be made to the extent allowed
by applicable federal and/or state law.
Non-Exempt Employees
Also known as hourly employees, are typically paid by the hour for all hours worked, are
eligible for overtime, and are subject to the provisions of federal and state wage and
hour laws. Non-Exempt employees must record the total hours worked and leave taken
each day. Mainstay has also established the following categories for non-exempt and
exempt employees:
● Regular Full-Time Employees are those normally scheduled to work 40 hours or
more per week. Upon date of hire, regular, full-time employees are eligible for all
12
Company-sponsored benefits.
● Regular Part-Time Employees are those who are normally scheduled to and work
less than 40 hours per week. Part-time employees may be eligible for
Mainstay-sponsored benefits, depending on the number of hours they are
regularly scheduled to work per week. All part-time employees are eligible for
benefits mandated by state, local and federal law. Please refer to current benefits
plan documents and Company policies for eligibility.
● Temporary Employees are those employed for short-term assignments and who
are not considered regular employees. A temporary employee’s status will
change only if the employee is advised of such a change, in writing, by the
People Team. Temporary employees are not eligible for any Company-sponsored
benefits except to the extent mandated by state, local and federal law.
k) Employee Records
Your personnel file consists of the employee's withholding forms, emergency
information and any performance appraisals, benefits data and other appropriate
employment-related documents including those included in a definition of personnel
records by applicable law. It is your responsibility to keep the personal data on file up to
date. Medical and safety records, if any, will be kept in a separate confidential file.
Personnel records are considered Company property. Current and former employees
may inspect and/or receive a copy of the employee’s personnel records that the
Company maintains relating to the employee’s performance or to any grievance
concerning them, in accordance with applicable law. Employees also have the right to
receive copies of documents in their file that they have previously signed that relate to
the obtaining or holding of employment. To the extent state law provides additional
access or rights to copies of personnel or payroll records, the Company shall comply
with applicable state law. Employees may not remove any document from their
personnel file; however, they may add their version of any disputed item to their file.
The request to inspect and/or copy an employee’s personnel file must be made in
writing by a current or former employee or their representative. The request to inspect
your personnel file must be submitted to the People Team at hr@mainstay.io.
l) Employee Privacy & Surveillance
Mainstay is committed to high standards of integrity with respect to protecting the
privacy of its employees, and to maintaining the accuracy, confidentiality, and security
of employee personal information. Accordingly, Mainstay handles employee personal
information with care and in accordance with all applicable laws. If you have questions
about employee privacy and handling of personal information, please contact the People
Team at hr@mainstay.io. Additionally, and in compliance with federal and state laws, the
13
Company may find it necessary to monitor work areas with security cameras for job-
,
business-
, or security-related reasons, and video footage collected may be used in
connection with disciplinary measures. Employees should not expect privacy in working
areas. Employee privacy in non-working areas will be respected to the extent possible.
Please contact your People Team if you have any questions about this policy.
m) Internal Transfers
Mainstay cares about providing our employees with every opportunity possible to help
them grow in their careers, wherever it may take them. To that end, Mainstay
understands that employees may wish to pursue other career opportunities and
development with the Company and strives to provide internal candidates with
meaningful career opportunities, development resources, and exposure to company
leadership.
The purpose of the Internal Transfer process is to provide clear direction on the steps to
take when considering an internal transfer that prioritizes an enhanced employee
experience. For more information, speak to a member of the People Team by emailing
hr@mainstay.io.
n) Corrective Action & Discipline
There are occasions when employees fail to meet performance standards or to abide by
Mainstay’s policies, procedures, and rules governing appropriate conduct. In order to
deal with such situations in a fair and consistent manner, the Company has developed a
disciplinary policy and procedure.
Corrective actions may include verbal, written, and final warnings, suspensions, and
termination. Corrective action is not necessarily a consecutive process, and corrective
actions can be imposed at any level without prior lesser corrective action. While
Mainstay attempts to utilize the principles of progressive discipline, the Company
reserves the right to impose appropriate discipline as determined in its sole discretion. In
determining the appropriate corrective action, the Company will consider the nature and
seriousness of the behavior or performance, the circumstances of the situation, any
prior corrective action, and other appropriate factors. A prior warning is not a
requirement for termination.
It should be remembered that employment is at the mutual consent of the Employee and
Mainstay. Accordingly, either the employee or Mainstay can terminate the employment
relationship at any time at will, with or without notice, with or without cause.
