consent of the Company and/or without notifying the company of the absence, will be
considered as having voluntarily resigned as a result of job abandonment, unless
applicable law requires otherwise. The company will make all reasonable efforts to
contact the employee during the three-day period of unnotified, unexcused absence.
If the employee is unable to contact the company for any absence, they should designate
a representative (such as a family member or friend) to do so on the employee's behalf. If
the employee or a representative is unable to contact Mainstay due to extreme
circumstances (such as a medical emergency or natural disaster that prohibits the
employee or their representative from contacting the company within three days), the
employee or their representative must contact the company as soon as practicable to
explain the situation.
This Policy will be applied in accordance with all applicable laws, including without
limitation the Family and Medical Leave Acts, Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)
(including leave provided as a reasonable accommodation under federal, state or local
law), National Labor Relations Act, Uniformed Services Employment and Reemployment
Rights Act, workers’ compensation acts, state and local mandatory paid sick leave laws
and ordinances, and other applicable federal, state and local laws.
c) Reasonable Accommodations Policy
Mainstay complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), Pregnant Workers
Fairness Act (PWFA), and all other applicable federal, state, and local laws that prohibit
discrimination against applicants and individuals with disabilities or known limitations
related to pregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions. The Reasonable
Accommodations Policy is in place to ensure we support our applicants and employees
who are dealing with either a physical or mental disability or known limitations related to
pregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions. Consistent with this commitment,
Mainstay will provide reasonable accommodations to disabled applicants and
employees if the reasonable accommodation would allow the individual to perform the
essential functions of the job, unless doing so would create an undue hardship or cause
a direct threat to the individual or others in the workplace. Likewise, Mainstay will
provide reasonable accommodations to individuals with limitations related to pregnancy,
childbirth, or related medical conditions unless doing so would create an undue
17
hardship.
Employees should contact the People Team with any questions or requests for
accommodation. Mainstay encourages employees to make their request in writing,
including the following information:
● A description of the accommodation the employee is requesting.
● The reason the employee needs an accommodation.
● How the accommodation will help the employee perform the essential
functions of the job.
Please submit accommodation or ergonomic requests (i.e. keyboards, ergo chairs, etc.)
using this Mainstay Reasonable Accommodation Request Form You will hear back from
the People team regarding next steps.
Upon receiving a request, the Company will engage in an interactive dialogue with the
employee to explore potential reasonable accommodations so that the employee can
perform the essential functions of their job. Mainstay is not required to make the specific
accommodation requested by the employee and may provide an alternative effective
accommodation.
Mainstay may ask the employee to provide supporting documents. If the information
provided by the employee is insufficient, Mainstay may require the employee to see a
healthcare professional of Mainstay’s choosing, at its expense. Mainstay will keep
confidential any medical information obtained in connection with an employee’s request
for reasonable accommodation. Individuals will not be retaliated against for requesting
an accommodation in good faith.
Mainstay prohibits any retaliation against an employee or applicant who complains in
good faith about a violation of this policy, requests an accommodation, or assists in any
investigation related to this policy. Any employee or applicant who believes that they
have been discriminated against on the basis of a disability/pregnancy, for requesting a
reasonable accommodation for a disability/pregnancy, or has experienced retaliation for
exercising their rights under federal, state, or local laws related to disabilities/pregnancy
and the workplace should notify the People team immediately. Mainstay will investigate
all complaints as soon as reasonably possible. Any employee, supervisor, or manager
who is found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and
including termination.
d) Religious Accommodations
The Company respects the religious beliefs and practices of all employees and will
make, on request, an accommodation for such observances when a reasonable
18
accommodation is available that does not create an undue hardship on Company’s
business.
An employee whose religious beliefs or practices conflict with his or her job, work
schedule, or with Mainstay’s policy or practice on dress and appearance, or with other
aspects of employment, and who seeks a religious accommodation must submit a
written request for the accommodation to the People Team through hr@mainstay.io as
far in advance as possible and cooperate with Mainstay in seeking and evaluating
accommodation alternatives. The written request will include the type of religious
conflict that exists and the employee’s suggested accommodation.
The request will be evaluated to determine whether a work conflict exists due to a
sincerely held religious belief or practice and whether an accommodation is available
that is reasonable and that would not create an undue hardship on the Company. An
accommodation may be a change in job, using paid leave or leave without pay, allowing
an exception to the dress and appearance code that does not affect safety
requirements, or for other aspects of employment. Employees will not be retaliated
against for requesting an accommodation.
e) Lactation Accommodations
Mainstay supports the legal right and necessity of employees who choose to express
milk in the workplace. An employee will be permitted reasonable break time to express
breast milk for nursing consistent with applicable federal, state, and local laws.
Employees who would like to request a lactation accommodation should contact their
People Team. An appropriate place will be provided for this purpose (not a restroom) in
close proximity to the employee’s work area for the employee to express milk in private.
The break time will, if possible, run concurrently with any break time already provided to
the employee. Break time that does not run concurrently with the break time already
provided to the employee will be unpaid except as otherwise provided by applicable law.
Employees must arrange all such breaks with their immediate supervisors.
Remote employees who are nursing mothers are entitled to reusable break time to
express milk for up to one year after birth. Employees may adjust their schedules to
accommodate lactation needs, in coordination with their manager. During video calls
employees are entitled to privacy and are not required to have their camera on while
pumping or breastfeeding.
Mainstay expressly prohibits retaliation against lactating employees for exercising any
rights granted under applicable law.
