g) Workplace Violence
It is every employee’s responsibility to assist in establishing and maintaining a
violence-free work environment. Therefore, each employee is expected and
encouraged to report any incident which may be threatening to you or your co-workers
or any event which you reasonably believe is threatening or violent. Please immediately
report any incident to any manager or the People team at hr@mainstay.io. If you observe
an incident or threat of violence (actual or perceived) that is immediate and serious,
20
IMMEDIATELY DIAL 9-1-1 and report it to the police.
A threat includes, but is not limited to, any indication of intent to harm a person or
damage personal or Company property. Threats may be direct or indirect, and they may
be communicated verbally or nonverbally. The Company has zero tolerance for acts of
violence and threats of violence. Without exception, acts and threats of violence are not
permitted.
You should feel free to report, without fear of retaliation, any condition that you believe
poses a safety, health or security risk in the workplace. We will investigate such reports
promptly and thoroughly and take appropriate corrective action to support this policy. All
such acts and threats, even those made in apparent jest, will be taken seriously, and will
lead to discipline up to and including termination.
h) Drug & Alcohol Policy
Illegal use, consumption, possession, or sale of drugs is prohibited in the workplace,
while performing work for Mainstay, and at work-related functions. Employees are also
prohibited from arriving to work, being on Company property, or performing work (on or
off Company property) under the influence of illegal drugs.
An employee’s proper use of legal drugs is not prohibited by this policy. It is each
employee’s responsibility to check with a physician regarding whether the use of any
medication may adversely affect performance or safety at work. Any employee taking
prescription drugs or over-the-counter drugs that may impair the employee’s ability to
perform their job functions safely and effectively is required to notify his/her supervisor
of the drug use before starting or continuing work. When making such a notification, the
employee should not disclose any underlying medical condition. An employee who is
using a drug that may impair the employee’s ability to perform their job functions safely
and effectively may be subject to further assessment. In such cases, the Company will
conduct an individualized assessment of the individual’s ability to perform the essential
functions of the job in question while utilizing such drugs before taking any further
action related to the employee’s employment.
Note that it is Mainstay’s intention to comply with all applicable federal, state, and local
laws. Where state and federal law differ, however, the Company will comply with federal
law, except where otherwise provided. For example, some state laws permit the use and
possession of marijuana for medical and/or non-medical purposes, but federal law does
not. In the absence of state law to the contrary, the Company considers marijuana to be
an illegal drug for purposes of this policy. Moreover, even if an individual’s use of
marijuana may otherwise be permissible under state law, the use or possession of
marijuana or being under the influence or impaired by marijuana on Company property
or while on Company business is strictly prohibited.
Mainstay does not prohibit employees from consuming alcohol at social or business
21
functions that are work related. However, employees may not consume alcohol to the
point where they endanger their own safety or the safety of others, or engage in
inappropriate behavior. You must call a sober driver, taxi, or car service, or use public
transportation, if you have been drinking at our office or at a work-related event.
i) Weapons In The Workplace
Mainstay prohibits all employees from possessing any weapons of any kind at the
workplace and while engaged in activities for Mainstay on-site at customer homes. For
purposes of this policy, the workplace is defined to include Mainstay's buildings and
outdoor areas, and where permitted under federal and applicable state law, it also
includes Mainstay’s parking lot except in jurisdictions where Mainstay’s policy does not
apply to the parking lot, such as Arizona, Florida, Georgia, Minnesota, Ohio, Tennessee,
Texas, and Utah. In these states, employees are permitted to store lawfully possessed
firearms and ammunition in their locked, privately-owned vehicles while parked at the
Company’s premises. Employees must comply with all applicable licensure, storage, and
other legal requirements.
Weapons include the usual implements of combat, mayhem, and personal violence, such
as firearms; guns, clubs; sharp and/or pointed objects, such as knives; explosive or
incendiary devices; noxious, caustic, or toxic chemicals; and any item with the potential
to inflict harm upon another. This list is illustrative only, and not exhaustive. You should
feel free to report, without fear of retaliation, any conduct that you believe violates this
policy. Mainstay will investigate such reports promptly and thoroughly and take
appropriate corrective action to support this policy, up to and including termination.
Please reach out to your manager or the People Team.
j) Dress Code
Mainstay strives to maintain a professional atmosphere that is conducive to our
business environment, promotes an inclusive environment for all employees, and
maintains a consistent professional appearance to our customers and colleagues. To
that end, we require our employees to dress appropriately and neatly both when they
are in-office or working remotely. Employees should exercise common sense and use
their best judgment regarding their clothing and appearance in the workplace and dress
in a manner that is consistent with the goals of this policy.
To assist you in this endeavor, here are a few guidelines to follow:
● Employees must always present a clean, professional appearance. All
employees should maintain an acceptable level of bodily hygiene to ensure
that interactions with other staff and clients remain positive and pleasant.
● Work clothes should be professional, which means they should not contain
profane language or be too revealing or distracting.
● Although we allow clothes with logos, attire with offensive or inappropriate
22
drawings, designs, or sayings are not permitted.
● The company has no policy against visible tattoos, but we urge our employees
to cover tattoos if they are offensive.
● This policy does not prohibit an employee from appearing or dressing
consistently with the employee’s gender identity or gender expression.
Any employee that requires reasonable accommodation for reasons based on religion,
disability, or other grounds protected by federal, state, or local laws should contact their
