PROHIBITING & PREVENTING DISCRIMINATION, HARASSMENT &
RETALIATION
Mainstay is committed to providing a work environment in which all individuals are treated
with respect and dignity. Everyone has the right to work in a professional atmosphere that
promotes equal employment opportunities and prohibits discriminatory practices,
including harassment and retaliation.
a) Harassment
Mainstay prohibits discrimination and harassment based on any of the following
categories: age, national origin/ancestry, color, marital or registered domestic partnership
status, reproductive health decision-making, medical condition, military or veteran’s
status (including unfavorable discharge from military service), national origin, ancestry,
mental or physical disability, race (including traits historically associated with race such
as hair texture and protective hairstyles), religion or creed (including observance and
practice and religious dress and grooming practices), sex (including pregnancy,
childbirth, breastfeeding or medical conditions related to breastfeeding, and medical
conditions related to pregnancy, childbirth or breastfeeding), gender, gender identify,
gender expression, sexual orientation, genetic characteristics/information, protective
order status, unfavorable discharge from military service, citizenship status, arrest
record, expunged or sealed convictions, or any other basis protected by federal, state or
local law or ordinance or regulation. Employees are also protected if they associate with a
person who is, or is perceived to be, in one of those classifications.
32
The Company’s policy applies to anyone involved in the operation of Mainstay and
prohibits unlawful harassment, discrimination and retaliation of any applicant or employee
(including, without limitation, unpaid interns) by any employee of the Company (including
managers, supervisors, and coworkers) and any third-party doing business or in a
professional relationship with the Company. This policy also prohibits unlawful
harassment against persons providing services to the Company pursuant to a contract.
Conduct prohibited by this policy is unacceptable in the workplace and in any
work-related setting, such as during business trips, business meetings, and
business-related social events.
Examples and the type of prohibited behaviors include, but are not limited to, the
following:
○
○
○
○
Verbal: suggestive, insulting or derogatory comments, epithets,
innuendoes, sounds, jokes, bullying, teasing or slurs based on any of the
above categories.
Physical: assault, impeding or blocking movement, or any unwanted
physical contact or interference with normal work or movement, including
touching, pinching, brushing the body, impeding or blocking movement,
contact or assault when directed at an individual because of any of the
above categories.
Visual: displaying derogatory posters, cartoons, or drawings, or making
derogatory gestures.
Online: derogatory statements or sexually suggestive postings in any social
media platform including Facebook, X (formerly Twitter), TikTok, Instagram,
Snapchat, etc.
b) Sexual Harassment
Sexual harassment is specific type of harassment and is prohibited by Mainstay. Sexual
harassment means any harassment based on someone's sex, sexual orientation, gender
identity, gender expression, or gender, as described in this policy. It includes harassment
that is not sexual in nature (e.g., offensive remarks about an individual's sex or gender),
as well as any unwelcome sexual advances or requests for sexual favors or any other
conduct of a sexual nature, when any of the following is true (unless applicable law
provides for different standards):
● Submission to the advance, request, or conduct is made either explicitly or
implicitly a term or condition of employment.
● Submission to or rejection of the advance, request, or conduct is used as a
basis for employment decisions.
● Such advances, requests, or conduct have the purpose or effect of
substantially or unreasonably interfering with an employee's work
performance by creating an intimidating, hostile, or offensive work
environment.
33
Sexually harassing behavior can be by a person of the same or opposite sex. Examples of
harassment include, but are not limited to:
● Verbal/written: Sexual innuendos, suggestive comments, jokes of a sexual
nature, sexual advances, sexual propositions, lewd remarks, or threats.
Requests for any type of sexual favor, including repeated, unwelcome requests
for dates. Unwanted sexual advances or propositions, such as: requests for
sexual favors accompanied by implied or overt threats concerning the target’s
job performance evaluation, a promotion, or other job benefits or detriments;
and/or subtle or obvious pressure for unwelcome sexual activities. This
includes conduct whether spoken or communicated over electronic means
(including telephone, voicemail, text message, email, social media (e.g.,
Facebook, Twitter/X, Instagram, TikTok, or Snapchat), or any other form of
electronic communication/application).
● Nonverbal: The distribution, display, or discussion of any written or graphic
material, including calendars, posters, pictures, graffiti, objects, promotional
material, reading materials, clothing or cartoons that are sexually suggestive,
sexually demeaning, pornographic, or shows hostility toward an individual or
group because of sex/gender; suggestive or insulting sounds; leering; staring;
whistling; or obscene gestures. This includes such sexual displays on
workplace computers or cell phones and sharing such displays while in the
workplace.
● Physical: Unwelcome, unwanted physical contact, including but not limited to,
touching, pinching, patting, brushing up against, hugging, cornering, kissing,
fondling, coerced sexual intercourse, or assault.
In addition, conduct based on any of the categories listed above, or any other
characteristic protected by law, is not appropriate for the workplace and is prohibited,
regardless of whether an individual makes a claim of prohibited behaviors.
c) Retaliation
Mainstay prohibits retaliation against anyone who in good faith reports harassment,
discrimination, or participates in any investigation of discrimination, harassment, or
retaliation. Anyone experiencing or witnessing any retaliatory or potentially retaliatory
conduct should report the conduct immediately, using the procedures outlined in the
reporting process below. Any report of retaliatory conduct will be immediately,
objectively, and thoroughly investigated in accordance with Company policy. If a
complaint of retaliation is substantiated, appropriate disciplinary action, up to and
including termination of employment, will be taken.
Retaliation means adverse conduct exhibited in reaction to an individual reporting an
34
actual or perceived violation of this policy, opposing practices prohibited by this policy, or
participating in the reporting and investigation process described below, or is otherwise
perceived as engaging in any of the above activity.
“Adverse conduct” includes but is not
limited to: any action that would keep an individual from reporting harassment or
retaliation; shunning and avoiding an individual who reports harassment or retaliation;
express or implied threats or intimidation intended to prevent an individual from reporting
harassment or retaliation; and denying employment benefits because an applicant or
employee reported harassment or retaliation or participated in the complaint reporting
and investigation process described below.
d) Reporting Harassment, Discrimination or Retaliation
The Company encourages reporting all perceived incidents of discrimination,
harassment, or retaliation. Any employee who believes they have been subjected to or
witnessed discrimination, harassment, or retaliation in violation of this policy should
immediately report the facts of the incident or incidents, the names of the individuals
involved, and the names of any witnesses to the People Team at hr@mainstay.io. If you
receive a complaint of harassment, discrimination or retaliation from a non-employee,
also immediately report the complaint to the People Team or by emailing
hr@mainstay.io.com. Mainstay prohibits retaliation against any employee for making a
good faith report of discrimination, harassment, or retaliation, or for participating in the
investigation of such a report.
Any manager or supervisor who receives a complaint or otherwise becomes aware of
conduct that may violate this policy must report the conduct to the People Team or by
emailing hr@mainstay.io.com so that an investigation can be made and corrective action
taken, if appropriate.
After a report is received, a timely, thorough, fair, and impartial investigation will be
undertaken by qualified employees of the Company or by an outside investigator. The
investigation will be conducted in a manner that will provide all parties appropriate due
process. It may include individual interviews with the parties involved and, where
necessary, with individuals who may have observed the alleged conduct or may have
other relevant knowledge. The investigation will be documented, and the Company will
track the investigation to make sure that it is progressing on a reasonable timeframe.
Complaints and investigations will be handled discreetly and in as confidential a manner
as possible, to the extent consistent with a full, fair, and proper investigation. However,
Mainstay cannot guarantee that all aspects of a complaint will always be kept
confidential, as sometimes Mainstay may need to reveal some information, for example,
to conduct a thorough investigation or to impose discipline.
The investigation will be completed, and determinations made as soon as practicable, and
Mainstay will reach reasonable conclusions based on the evidence collected in the
investigation. The results of the investigation will be communicated to the complaining
35
party as soon as possible.
If Mainstay determines that a violation of this policy has occurred, effective remedial
action will be taken in accordance with the circumstances involved. Any employee who
has been found by Mainstay to have violated this policy will be subject to discipline up to
and including termination. Because a work environment free of harassment,
discrimination and retaliation is so important, Mainstay may take disciplinary action
against an employee who exhibits poor judgment or engages in inappropriate behavior,
including sexually inappropriate conduct, even if the conduct falls short of constituting
unlawful harassment, discrimination or retaliation.
In addition to our internal complaint procedure, employees may also contact either the
Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) or the local enforcement agency in
their state to report unlawful harassment. The EEOC serves as neutral factfinders and will
attempt to assist the parties to voluntarily resolve their disputes. For more information,
contact the People Team at hr@mainstay.io or see the applicable state specific
information.
e) Training
Mainstay provides sexual harassment prevention and respect in the workplace training to
employees in accordance with applicable federal, state and local laws.
If you have any questions concerning this policy, please feel free to contact your
supervisor, the People Team at hr@mainstay.io, or any of Mainstay's managers.
ACCEPTABLE USE OF TECHNOLOGY & INFORMATION SYSTEMS
Mainstay is committed to protecting the assets, information, systems, and personnel that
are critical to achieving its business objectives. These protections may be governed by
legal, contractual, or company policy considerations. The purpose of this Policy is to
establish and communicate requirements for the acceptable use of Mainstay’s assets,
technology and information systems. This Policy is aligned with, and supports, the
Mainstay Information Security Policy. It is the responsibility of any person using Mainstay
information or systems to read, understand, and follow Mainstay’s Acceptable Use Policy,
Information Security Policy, and Password Policy.
a) Scope
This Policy covers the use of all assets, technology, information, and other system
components that are owned, operated, maintained, or controlled by Mainstay. This Policy
applies to all Mainstay Employees, Independent contractors, and third parties, as
applicable, with access to Mainstay non-public information, systems, and locations,
Non-public information, regardless of format. System Components including Information
36
systems, Owned and operated infrastructure, Leveraged cloud infrastructure and systems
regardless of the provider. In cases where provider policy is not aligned with this Policy,
the stricter policy is applicable. All other system hardware and software, both internally
and externally, that interact with these systems, and all other relevant systems.
b) Requirements
Company assets including information, systems, and system components must be used
legally and primarily for business purposes. Personnel are responsible for exercising
good judgment regarding the reasonableness of personal use of Mainstay systems
including:
All Mainstay non-public information transmitted to, sent from, received, or printed from,
or stored or recorded on systems, whether owned or leased by Mainstay, an employee, or
a third party, is the sole property of Mainstay. All Mainstay issued assets must be
returned as soon as possible on request or separation from the Company.
Expectation of Privacy
While Mainstay maintains a reasonable level of privacy, personnel should have no
expectation of privacy when using Company assets and Mainstay reserves the right to
monitor, intercept, record, and review any activity happening on Company systems.
Acceptable Use
All Mainstay information must be handled in accordance with the Mainstay Information
Classification and Handling Guidelines, the Information Security Policy, and all other
applicable policies and procedures. It is unacceptable for any person using Mainstay
information or systems to:
1. Circumvent Security or Privacy Controls to include the tampering with,
circumventing, or removal of any security or privacy controls that are in place
to protect Mainstay information and systems is forbidden. Personnel are
expected to notify IT immediately of any suspected failure of such controls.
2. Installing Unauthorized Software or Hardware which includes the installation of
applications or hardware not intended for business purposes on Mainstay
systems is prohibited. All applications or hardware installations must be
authorized by IT for use.
3. Infringe Upon the Rights of Any Third Party: Mainstay assets must not be used
to infringe upon or violate the rights of third parties or otherwise put at risk a
third party’s non-public information or assets.
4. Contribute to Any Non-Business Purpose: Mainstay assets must not be used to
engage in any non-Mainstay commercial, political, religious, or illegal
endeavor. Mainstay assets, technology and systems are for business use
only. Any non-business use is prohibited.
5. Infringe Upon the Rights of Any Individual: Mainstay assets must not be used to
engage in threatening or harassing activities or otherwise contribute to the
37
distribution of inappropriate, libelous or defamatory materials or messages.
Sending, posting, recording, or encouraging the receipt of any information
that may be offensive because of sexual, racist, or religious content, or
considered discriminatory or harassing based on any characteristic protected
under any applicable law is forbidden.
6. Access Inappropriate Information: Mainstay assets must not be used to access
or seek to access information deemed by Mainstay to be offensive or
detrimental to conducting Company business. Such information includes, but
is not limited to, gambling sites, sexually explicit content, information that
promotes violence or harassment, or sites and content that could disrupt
Mainstay business or would otherwise constitute a violation of Mainstay
policies.
7. Access Unauthorized Information: Mainstay assets must not be used to attempt
to access or seek to access any non-public information or systems for which
there is no business justification to access. Access to assets and information
must be granted by the information owner or their authorized designee.
8. Damage the Mainstay Brand or Mission: Mainstay assets must not be used to
engage in or encourage any activity that could negatively impact the
Mainstay brand or mission.
9. Expose Confidential Non-Public Information: Mainstay assets must not be used
to reveal non-public business information, including official Mainstay
information or intellectual property without authorization. Mainstay
information classifications can be found in the Mainstay Information
Classification and Handling Guidelines.
10. Misrepresent Themselves: Mainstay assets must not be used to facilitate the
misrepresentation of an individual as another individual or organization or to
speak on behalf of Mainstay without authorization.
11. Interfere with Mainstay’s Business or Personnel: Mainstay assets must not be
used to engage in or encourage any activity that could negatively impact the
