d) Exempt Employee Flex Time Off (FTO)
The Company provides flexible paid time off (Flexible PTO) benefits to regular, full-time
and reduced-schedule exempt US employees who are regularly scheduled to work at
least 30 hours per week. Flexible PTO may be used for any reason, including any reason
covered by an applicable paid sick leave law.
Regular, full-time exempt employees will be entitled to take Flexible PTO, without loss of
pay, up to fifteen (15) consecutive business days at a time dependent on manager
approval. There is no limit to the amount of Flexible PTO an eligible employee may take in
a single year.
Flexible PTO is not a form of additional wages for services performed, but is part of
Mainstay’s promise to provide a flexible work schedule—including employees’ ability to
decide when and how much time to take off. Because Flexible PTO is not accrued,
earned, or tracked, there is no concept of “unused” Flexible PTO, it will not carry over
from one year to the next, and there will be no payout of Flexible PTO upon separation of
employment.
We trust that employees eligible for Flexible PTO will exercise good judgment in
considering their responsibilities to the Company and in consulting with their managers to
determine reasonable time off before requesting Flexible PTO, except in cases of illness,
injury or other emergency. Employees should avoid taking Flexible PTO that negatively
impact their job and the Company. Employees should coordinate with their team
members in advance of anticipated time off to ensure that work is covered and remains
on track.
Reasonable efforts will be made to approve requests; however, with the exception of
requests for reasons covered by an applicable paid sick leave law or for a legally
protected leave of absence, the Company reserves the right to deny Flexible PTO
requests in its sole discretion, including but not limited to, if the timing of the request
does not satisfy business operational needs or will result in inadequate staffing levels due
43
to other employees taking Flexible PTO at the same time. Managers will approve
reasonable Flexible PTO requests based on legitimate business reasons (such as the
employee’s productivity, progress the employee has made on his or her goals, the status
of projects, and fairness to the employee’s coworkers).
Employees and managers should communicate openly and use common sense when
requesting or approving Flexible PTO so the policy is applied fairly and equitable amongst
all eligible employees.
