ALABAMA EMPLOYEES ONLY
Jury Duty Leave
Alabama employees who are summoned to serve on a jury are entitled to paid leave for
any days that they are required to serve as juror. Employees eligible to take leave under
this policy shall be entitled to the payment of their usual compensation. Employees are not
required to use their accrued PTO during this leave. To be eligible for leave under this
policy, the employee must present their supervisor with the summons for jury duty on the
workday following receipt of the summons. Employees must report for work on their next
regularly scheduled hour after being dismissed from a jury.
Volunteer Firefighter or Emergency Medical Service Provider Leave
Employees who are volunteer firefighters or emergency medical service providers may
take leave to respond to an emergency call prior to the time the employee is due to work.
Time lost may be charged against the employee for missing work. Please consult the
People Team for additional details.
ALASKA EMPLOYEES ONLY
Leave for Crime Victims
An employee who has been a victim of crime or whose family member has been killed or
65
incapacitated as a result of a crime may take leave to respond to a subpoena to testify in a
criminal proceeding or to participate in the investigation of the offense by a law
enforcement agency. Please consult the People Team at hr@mainstay.io for additional
details.
ARIZONA EMPLOYEES ONLY
Paid Sick Leave
Mainstay provides eligible employees with paid sick time pursuant to the Arizona Fair
Wages and Healthy Families Act (FWHFA). Eligible associates working in the state of
Arizona accrue one hour of paid sick time for every 30 hours worked, up to the applicable
maximum accrual cap, beginning on the hire date. Exempt associates accrue at the same
rate for 40 hours per workweek, but if the associate’s normal work week is less than 40
hours, then sick time accrued is based on the number of hours they normally work per
week. Accrued, unused paid leave will be carried over from year to year. However, once
the maximum amount of time has been accrued, no further sick and safe leave will accrue
until previously accrued leave is used. Compensation for accrued and unused paid sick
time is not provided upon separation from employment for any reason.
Discriminating or retaliating against an associate for doing any of the following is
prohibited: (1) asserting any claim or right under the FWHFA, including requesting or using
earned paid sick time; (2) assisting any person in doing so; or (3) informing any person of
their rights under the FWHFA.
Each associate has the right to file a complaint with the Industrial Commission’s Labor
Department alleging that an employer has violated the FWHFA. For additional information
regarding the FWHFA, associates may refer to the Industrial Commission’s website at
www.azica.gov or contact the Industrial Commission’s Labor Department: 800 W.
Washington, Phoenix, Arizona 85007-2022; (602) 542-4515.
Voluntary Open Door Policy
Employees are encouraged, but are not required, to communicate with Mainstay
whenever you are considering resigning because you believe working conditions may
have become intolerable. However, Arizona law requires employees to notify their
company in writing when such a working condition exists that is believed to be intolerable,
that would compel the employee to resign, or that constitutes a constructive discharge, if
that employee wants to preserve the right to bring a claim against the employer alleging
that the working condition forced the employee to resign.
To preserve this claim, an employee must wait 15 calendar days after providing written
notice before resigning from employment. Depending upon the circumstances, an
66
employee may be entitled to a paid or unpaid leave of absence of up to 15 days while
waiting for Mainstay to respond to written communication about working conditions. Such
concerns should be reported to the People Team or by emailing hr@mainstay.io.
CALIFORNIA EMPLOYEES ONLY
Sexual Harassment Prevention Training
Every two years, all employees will attend sexual harassment prevention and respect in
the workplace training aimed at increasing their understanding of and preventing
workplace harassment (including harassment on the basis of sexual orientation, gender
identity, and gender expression) and their role in creating an underlying culture of mutual
respect in the workplace. Newly hired employees will receive training within six months of
assuming a position with Mainstay. Employees may also view the sexual harassment
online training course created by the California Civil Rights Department at
https://calcivilrights.ca.gov/shpt.
Reporting Discrimination, Harassment, and Retaliation
In California, employees may address their harassment, discrimination, or retaliation
complaints to the California Civil Rights Department and/or the Equal Employment
Opportunity Commission.The law protects employees against retaliation for filing a
complaint with either the EEOC or CRD or otherwise participating in an investigation,
proceeding, or hearing conducted by either agency.
California Family Rights Act
California employees who have worked at Mainstay for a total of at least 12 continuous
months and who have worked at least 1,250 hours during the twelve months immediately
prior to the start of the leave, may be eligible for unpaid leave under the California Family
Rights Act (“CFRA”), which coincides with Mainstay’s FMLA policy.
Eligible employees may take leave:
○
To bond with a new child by birth, adoption or foster care placement, within
one year of the child’s birth, adoption or foster placement.
To care for a child, spouse, domestic partner, parent, grandparent,
grandchild, sibling or designated person with a serious health condition.
CFRA may also be taken for the employee’s own serious health condition.
For a qualifying exigency related to the covered active duty or call to cover
active duty of a qualifying family member in the Armed Forces of the United
States.
○
○
67
●
Eligible employees may take leave up to 12 work weeks in a 12-month period,
proportional to an employee’s normal work schedule. CFRA generally runs
concurrently with FMLA leave.
CFRA leave does not need to be taken in one continuous period of time. Leave taken
because of a serious health condition may be taken intermittently (in separate blocks of
time due to a single health condition) or on a reduced-leave schedule (reducing the usual
number of hours you work per workweek or workday) if medically necessary and
reasonably accommodated. Employees may also be eligible for intermittent leave for the
birth or placement of a child.
Eligible employees may take leave on a full-time, intermittent or reduced schedule basis.
In certain situations in which intermittent leave or an alternative work schedule leave is
required, Mainstay may temporarily transfer the employee to another job, with equivalent
pay and benefits, to better accommodate such leave.
Whenever the need for leave is foreseeable, employees must submit written requests for
such leave at least 30 days in advance. If the need for leave is unforeseeable, employees
must provide notice to Mainstay at the earliest available time. Similar to FMLA leave,
Mainstay requires certification from an employee’s health care provider to substantiate a
CFRA leave for the employee’s own serious health condition, or certification from the
health care provider of the employee’s family member who has a serious health condition
to substantiate a CFRA leave to take care of that family member. The employee and
healthcare provider should not disclose or provide information regarding the employee’s
family medical history, genetic information, or other protected health information. Failure
to provide requested medical certification in a timely manner may be grounds for delay or
denial of leave.
Although leave under CFRA is unpaid, employees may be eligible for wage reimbursement
benefits under Short Term Disability Insurance or Paid Family Leave. Similarly, leave taken
under FMLA is unpaid but employees may be eligible for state disability insurance through
the Employment Development Department, also known as the EDD. State disability
insurance provides eligible employees with partial wage benefits when they are unable to
work due to non-work related illness, injury or pregnancy.
If State Disability Insurance is not applicable, employees on CFRA leave are required to
first substitute any accrued paid leave for the otherwise unpaid portions of CFRA leave.
Under CFRA, employees may also be able to substitute sick time for up to a maximum of
two weeks in an applicable 24-month period to attend to a serious health condition of a
covered individual other than the employee.
Except for circumstances unrelated to the taking of CFRA leave, an employee who returns
to work at the Company at the expiration of leave is entitled to return to the same position
formerly held or an equivalent position with equivalent pay and benefits.
68
It is the Employee’s responsibility to notify the Company of his/her intention to return to
work from a leave of absence. Please contact the People Team at hr@mainstay.io at least
30 days prior to your return to work date. If the leave of absence was for the serious
health condition of an employee, written medical certification from the employee’s health
care provider is required, and must include:
• Certification of the employee’s fitness to return to work, and the date the release is
effective
• Information regarding restrictions/limitations that must be followed, along with the
anticipated time period for which these restrictions/limitations should be observed
(if any).
Failure to provide written medical certification by the health care provider of the
employee’s fitness to return to work will result in denial of reinstatement for the employee
until certification is obtained.
If an employee fails to return to work at the expiration of a leave and has not requested
and been approved for an extension or their leave of absence or other reasonable
accommodation, the employee will be considered to have resigned.
During the period of an employee’s approved family or medical leave, Mainstay will
continue to pay its portion of medical insurance premiums for the employee to the same
extent as it did prior to the employee taking such leave. If the employee does not return to
work at the end of the leave, Mainstay reserves the right to demand reimbursement for
the premiums paid during the leave, unless the employee’s failure to return to work is due
to medical circumstances beyond the employee’s control.
All questions regarding CFRA leave should be addressed to the People Team.
Pregnancy Disability Leave
Pregnancy disability leave (“PDL”) is available for employees with disabilities related to
pregnancy, childbirth or a related medical condition.
“Disabled” as used in this section
includes, but is not limited to, severe morning sickness, prenatal or postnatal care, bed
rest, gestational diabetes, pregnancy-induced hypertension, preeclampsia, postpartum
depression, childbirth, loss or end of pregnancy, and/or recovery from childbirth or loss or
end of pregnancy. Only your healthcare provider (as defined by California law) may
determine whether you are disabled.
An employee may take up to four (4) months of leave per pregnancy regardless of length
of service with Mainstay. The four-month leave period is equivalent to the number of
hours an employee would regularly work in the four (4) month period.
An employee can take PDL at any time that the employee’s healthcare provider designates
the employee as disabled by pregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions.
69
Conditions for which PDL is available include, but are not limited to, severe morning
sickness, prenatal or postnatal care, doctor ordered bed rest, pregnancy-induced
hypertension, preeclampsia, postpartum depression, and loss or end of pregnancy. The
leave need not be taken at once. The pregnant employee can take PDL intermittently, as
needed. PDL will run concurrently with FMLA leave, where applicable, but does not run
concurrently with CFRA leave.
Except in cases of emergency, employees must provide Mainstay with advance notice of
the need for leave or other accommodation related to pregnancy. If the need for leave is
foreseeable, employees must provide notice at least 30 days before the leave is to begin.
If 30 days’ advance notice is not possible due to a lack of knowledge of when the leave,
reasonable accommodation, or transfer will begin, a change in circumstances, a medical
emergency, or other good cause, employees must notify Mainstay as soon as possible.
Any employee requesting PDL will be required to provide medical certification from the
employee’s health care provider reflecting that the employee is no longer able to work due
to a pregnancy-related disability. The certification should include:
1. The date on which the employee became disabled due to pregnancy or the date
of the medical advisability of transfer;
2. The probable duration of the period(s) of disability or the period(s) for the
advisability of transfer; and
3. A statement that, due to disability, the employee is either unable to work at or
perform any one or more of the essential functions of her position without
undue risk to herself or other persons; or a statement that, due to the
employee’s pregnancy, a transfer to an existing and open less strenuous or
hazardous position or duties is medically advisable to the extent it may be
reasonably accommodated.
PDL leave is unpaid by Mainstay. Mainstay requires employees on PDL to use all available
California paid sick leave. Employees may elect to use accrued vacation or other accrued
paid time off as part of their PDL before taking the remainder of the leave on an unpaid
basis. Employees may also be eligible for state disability benefits during the unpaid
portion of PDL.
When PDL ends, the employee will be reinstated to the same position, unless either the job
ceases to exist because of legitimate business reasons or the means of preserving the job
would substantially undermine the ability of Mainstay to operate safely and/or efficiently.
In most instances, if the same position is not available, the employee will be offered a
comparable position in terms of pay, benefits, job content, and promotional opportunities.
Employees are also entitled to reasonable accommodations for medical needs related to
pregnancy, childbirth or related conditions, including but not limited to, a transfer to a less
strenuous or hazardous position (where one is available) or duties if medically needed
because of pregnancy.
70
Paid Family Leave
Paid Family Leave (“PFL”) is a California program offering up to eight (8) weeks of wage
reimbursement in any 12-month period to (1) care for a seriously ill family member; (2)
bond with a new child; or (3) participate in a qualifying event because of a family
member’s military deployment. While PFL provides wage replacement benefits, it does not
create an independent right to a leave of absence or provide job protection.
PFL is administered by the Employment Development Department (EDD), which directly
receives any application for PFL benefits, and which directly pays benefits to eligible
employees. The EDD, not Mainstay, determines whether any absence qualifies an
employee for benefits, and the EDD can require documentation in support of this claim.
PFL shall run concurrently with an employee’s FMLA/CFRA leaves of absence, where
applicable.
Leave for Victims of Domestic Violence & Sexual Assault
To the extent required by law, employees who are victims of domestic violence or of
sexual assault may receive unpaid leave to: 1) obtain services from a domestic violence
shelter or rape crisis center; 2) seek medical attention for injuries caused by domestic
violence or sexual assault; 3) obtain psychological counseling for the domestic violence
or sexual assault; 4) take action, such as relocation, to protect against future domestic
violence or sexual assault; or 5) obtain a restraining order, a temporary restraining order,
or other injunctive relief to protect against future domestic violence, stalking, or sexual
assault.
To take this leave, the employee must provide the Company with advance notice of this
leave. If advance notice is not possible, the employee must provide the Company with the
following certification upon returning back to work: 1) a police report showing that the
employee was a victim of domestic violence or sexual assault; or 2) a court order
protecting the employee from the perpetrator or other evidence from the court or
prosecuting attorney that the employee appeared in court; or 3) documentation from a
medical professional, domestic violence or sexual assault victim advocate, health care
provider, or counselor showing that the employee’s absence was due to treatment for
injuries from domestic violence or sexual assault.
The employee may choose to use any accrued personal time off, if available, for an
absence described above.
The Company is also committed to complying with the laws protecting victims of domestic
violence, sexual assault or stalking by providing reasonable accommodation(s) to protect
the safety of the victim while at work. The Company will provide a reasonable
71
accommodation for any known victim of domestic violence, sexual assault or stalking to
the extent required by law, provided the requested accommodation does not create an
undue hardship for the Company and/or does not pose a direct threat to the health or
safety of others in the workplace and/or to the individual. If you require accommodation
for your safety while at work, you must notify the President. Once the Company is aware
of the need for an accommodation, the Company will engage in an interactive process to
identify possible accommodations.
Crime Victims Leave for Judicial Proceedings
Mainstay provides employees who are the victim of a crime (or the family member of a
victim of a crime) with unpaid leave in order to attend judicial proceedings related to the
crime or the rights of the victim. A family member under this policy includes a spouse,
domestic partner, child, stepchild, brother, stepbrother, sister, stepsister, mother,
stepmother, father, or stepfather.
When the need for leave is foreseeable, you must provide documentation of the
scheduled proceeding. Such notice is typically given to the victim of the crime by a court
or government agency setting the hearing, a district attorney or prosecuting attorney's
office, or a victim/witness office. If advance notice is not possible, you must provide
appropriate documentation within a reasonable time after the absence.
Any absence from work to attend judicial proceedings will be unpaid, unless you choose
to take paid time off, such as accrued PTO.
Mainstay will not retaliate against employees who request or take leave in accordance
with this policy.
Mainstay will maintain the confidentiality of any records regarding an employee’s absence
from work pursuant to this policy.
Reproductive Loss Leave
Employees are entitled to up to five (5) days of reproductive loss leave following a
reproductive loss event. A “reproductive loss event” means the day or, for a multiple-day
event, the final day of a failed adoption, failed surrogacy, miscarriage, stillbirth, or an
unsuccessful assisted reproduction.
Leave must be taken within three (3) months of the event, except if, prior to or
immediately following a reproductive loss event, an employee is on or chooses to go on
leave from work pursuant to the CFRA, the PDL, or any other leave entitlement under state
or federal law, the employee shall complete their reproductive loss leave within three
months of the end date of the other leave, and pursuant to any existing leave policy of
72
Mainstay. The days an employee takes for reproductive loss leave may be
nonconsecutive.
If an employee experiences more than one reproductive loss event within a 12-month
period, the total amount of reproductive loss leave an employee is allowed to take is 20
days within a 12-month period. Reproductive loss leave is unpaid; however, employees
may use accrued and available paid sick leave.
Leave taken pursuant to this policy is separate from any leave right under the California
Fair Employment and Housing Act and will not run concurrently with any leave taken for
CFRA.
The Company will not retaliate against any employee because of their exercise of the right
to reproductive loss leave or the giving of information or testimony as to reproductive loss
leave. The Company will maintain employee confidentiality relating to leave taken under
this policy. Leave under this policy will not run concurrently with any leave taken for CFRA.
Paid Sick Leave
Employees in California have a right to paid sick leave as set forth under the California
Healthy Workplaces Healthy Family Act of 2014. Employees’ paid leave benefits under the
Company’s
Paid Sick Leave policy and are consistent with the requirements of California law, except
as further supplemented herein.
All full-time, part-time and temporary employees in California are eligible to take paid sick
leave in accordance with Mainstay’s Paid Sick Leave policy. Part-time employees will be
granted paid sick leave on a pro rata basis. In no event will an employee’s annual allotment
of paid sick leave fail to equal or exceed one (1) hour of paid sick leave for every 30 hours
worked, up to the applicable maximum accrual cap. For purposes of this policy, exempt
employees are deemed to work the lesser of 40 hours per workweek of their normal
workweek.
Eligible employees may designate one person for whom the employee may use paid sick
time to provide aid or care. A designated person means any individual related by blood or
whose association with the employee is the equivalent of a family relationship. The
designated person may be identified by the employee at the time the employee requests
the leave. Employees are limited to one designated person per 12-month period.
For non-exempt employees, paid sick leave is paid at the employee’s regular rate of pay
for the workweek in which the employee takes paid sick leave. For exempt employees,
paid sick leave is calculated based on the employee’s salary at the time paid sick leave is
73
taken.
If an employee separates from Mainstay and is rehired within one year, then previously
accrued, unused paid sick time will be reinstated. The employee is entitled to use such
paid sick leave immediately and to accrue additional paid sick time (subject to the
applicable maximum accrual cap) upon rehire.
If an employee separates from Mainstay and is rehired more than one year later, then
previously accrued, unused paid sick time is not reinstated. Instead, the employee is
treated as a new hire under this policy.
Some California cities have local paid sick leave ordinances, such as San Francisco,
Oakland, San Diego, Los Angeles and Berkeley. Mainstay will comply with both state and
local laws, and where they differ, will apply whichever one is more beneficial to the
employee.
Organ and Bone Marrow Donor Leave
Employees are entitled to up to thirty (30) business days of paid time off in any one-year
period for the purpose of serving as an organ to another person. An employee may also
take up to five (5) business days of paid time off to donate bone marrow to another
person. Employees must be employed by the Company for at least 90 days immediately
preceding the commencement of leave and request leave in writing. Employees serving as
an organ donor are eligible for an additional unpaid leave of absence of up to 30 business
days in a one-year period, following the 30 business days of paid leave.
When available, you must utilize up to five days sick or PTO time for initial bone marrow
donation leave and up to two weeks for initial organ donation leave.
Leave under this policy will not run concurrently with any leave taken for FMLA or CFRA.
In order to receive the leave of absence, you must provide a written medical certification.
Lactation Accommodation Policy
Mainstay will provide a reasonable amount of break time to accommodate an employee
desiring to express breast milk for the employee’s infant child. If possible, the break time
should be taken concurrently with other break periods already provided. If this time
cannot run concurrently with meal and rest periods already provided to the employee, the
break time will be unpaid if the employee is non-exempt. Exempt employees will be paid
as required by law.
The lactation area or room will be a permanent or temporary location, other than a
restroom, in close proximity to the employee’s work area for the employee to express milk
74
in private. Whether permanent or temporary, the room or area will be private, shielded
from view, free from intrusion, and used only for lactation purposes when the employee is
using the room for such purposes. The room or location will be safe, clean, have a surface
on which to place a breast pump and personal items, have electricity or access to
electricity and a place to sit.
The room or location may be the employee’s private office, if applicable. If because of
operational, financial or space limitations, Mainstay is unable to provide a permanent
lactation room or area, it will provide a temporary room or area.
Mainstay will provide access to a sink with running water and a refrigerator (or other
cooling device, if a refrigerator is not possible) suitable for storing milk, in close proximity
to the employee’s workplace.
Employees should notify the People Team if they are requesting time to express breast
milk under this policy or have questions about this policy. Mainstay will respond to a
request for a lactation accommodation within five (5) business days. If Mainstay is unable
to provide lactation accommodations, it will provide the employee with a written response
that identifies the basis for the denial.
Employees have a right to report violations of lactation accommodation requirements to
the California Labor Commissioner. The Company does, however, reserve the right to
deny an employee’s request for a lactation break if the additional break time will seriously
disrupt operations.
Mainstay will not tolerate discrimination, harassment or retaliation against an employee
based on the request for or usage of lactation accommodations.
Overtime
Mainstay will pay overtime to non-exempt employees at the rate of 1.5 times the
employee’s regular rate of pay for all hours worked over 40 hours in a workweek or eight
(8) hours in a workday. In addition, Mainstay will pay 1.5 times a non-exempt employee’s
regular rate of pay for the first eight (8) hours worked on the employee’s seventh (7th)
consecutive day of work in a workweek.
Mainstay pays overtime to non-exempt employees at the rate of two (2) times the
employee’s regular rate of pay for hours worked over 12 hours in a workday. In addition,
Mainstay will pay overtime at the rate of two (2) times the regular rate of pay for hours
worked over eight (8) hours on the employee’s seventh (7th) consecutive day of work in a
workweek.
Meal and Rest Periods
75
Non-exempt employees who work more than five (5) hours in a day are provided the
opportunity to take a 30-minute, uninterrupted, duty-free meal break commencing no later
than the end of their fifth hour of work after the start of their shift. Non-exempt employees
who work more than five (5) hours in a day but no more than six (6) hours may waive
being provided the opportunity to take a meal period. This can be done only with the
mutual consent of the employee and an authorized member of Mainstay’s management,
and Mainstay may require that your waiver be in writing.
Non-exempt employees who work more than ten (10) hours in a day are provided the
opportunity to take a second 30-minute, uninterrupted, duty-free meal break no later than
the end of their tenth hour of work after the start of their shift. A non-exempt employee
can waive being provided the opportunity to take the second meal period if their hours
worked do not exceed twelve (12) hours, there is mutual consent between the employee
and an authorized member of Mainstay’s management, and there was not a waiver of their
first meal break period.
Non-exempt employees must clock out at the start of their meal periods and clock back
in at the end of their meal periods when ready to resume working.
All hourly, non-exempt employees are also authorized and permitted to take a ten (10)
minute paid rest break for every four (4) hours of work or major fraction thereof (defined
as any amount of time over two (2) hours). A rest break need not be authorized and
permitted for employees whose total daily work time is less than three and one half (3.5)
hours. If you work a shift from three and one-half (3.5) to six (6) hours in length, you will
be entitled to one (1) ten-minute rest break; if you work more than six (6) hours and up to
10 hours, you will be entitled to two (2) ten-minute rest breaks; and so on for subsequent
four hour work periods. These breaks should be taken as close to the middle of each
four-hour period insofar as operationally practicable.
Non-exempt employees must not clock out while taking authorized and permitted rest
breaks, which is compensable time.
Employees are relieved of all work-related duties and may leave the Company’s premises
during their meal and rest breaks. Employees may not combine meal or rest periods and
may not work through a meal period or rest break in order to come in late or leave early. If
for any reason you are not able to take your meal period or rest breaks or are discouraged
from or not permitted to do so by supervisory employees and/or operational conditions,
you must immediately notify your manager or supervisor or the People Team and explain
why you were unable to take any rest break or meal period.
An employee who refuses to take their breaks or meal period in accordance with this
policy may be subject to discipline, up to and including termination of employment.
