San Francisco Public Health Emergency Leave Policy (San Francisco Employees
Only)
Eligibility
Employees who work in the City or County of San Francisco are eligible for San Francisco
Public Health Emergency Leave (“PHEL”) as set forth below. This includes full-time,
part-time, and temporary employees.
Public Health Emergency Leave
Eligible employees may only use PHEL during a “Public Health Emergency,
” which means
a local or statewide health emergency related to any contagious, infectious, or
communicable disease, declared by the City of San Francisco’s local health officer or the
California State health officer pursuant to the California Health and Safety Code, or an Air
Quality Emergency, which is when the Bay Air Quality Management District issues a
“Spare the Air” Alert.
Amount of Public Health Emergency Leave
On January 1 of each year, the Company will allocate PHEL to each employee as
follows:
● Full-Time Employees: 80 hours.
● Non Full-Time Employees on a Fixed Regular Schedule: the number of hours the
employee regularly works or takes paid leave over a two-week period, not to exceed
80 hours.
● Variable Schedule Employees: the average number of hours over a two-week
period the employee worked or took paid leave over the previous calendar year or
since their start date if after the beginning of the previous calendar year, not to
exceed 80 hours.
79
PHEL is in addition to any regular paid time off that the Company offers such as paid sick
time, vacation, or PTO. However, PHEL will run concurrently with any other supplemental
paid sick leave related to a public health issue to the extent permitted by law.
Reasons for Use
Employees may use PHEL when they are unable to work (or telework) due to the following:
1. Public Health Recommendation or Requirement: The recommendations or
requirements of an individual or general federal, state, or local health order
(including an order issued by the local jurisdiction in which an Employee or a Family
Member* the Employee is caring for resides) related to a Public Health Emergency.
2. Quarantine or Isolation: the employee, or a Family Member* the employee is caring
for, has been advised by a Healthcare Provider to isolate or quarantine during a
Public Health Emergency.
3. Symptoms or Diagnosis: the employee, or a Family Member* the employee is caring
for, is experiencing symptoms of and seeking a medical diagnosis, or has received
a positive medical diagnosis, for a possible infectious, contagious, or
communicable disease associated with the Public Health Emergency.
4. School or Care Closure: the employee is caring for a Family Member* if the school
or place of care of the Family Member* has been closed, or the care provider of
such Family Member* is unavailable, due to the Public Health Emergency.
5. Spare the Air Alert: an Air Quality Emergency, if (a) the employee is a member of a
“Vulnerable Population” (meaning they have been diagnosed with a heart or lung
disease; have respiratory problems including but not limited to asthma,
emphysema, or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; are pregnant; or are age 60
or older) and (b) the employee primarily works outdoors. To use PHEL during a
Spare the Air Alert, you will be required to provide medical documentation
confirming that you are a member of a “Vulnerable Population.
”
*Family Member includes an employee’s: child; parent; legal guardian or ward; sibling;
grandparent; grandchild; and spouse, registered domestic partner under any state or local
law, or designated person (only available if the employee has no spouse or registered
domestic partner; only one “designated person” is permitted per year).
Increments of Use - PHEL may be used in increments as small as one hour.
Notice and Documentation - When requesting to use PHEL, please follow the Company’s
usual call-in procedures for foreseeable leave.
The Company may require a doctor’s note or other documentation to confirm an
employee’s status as a member of a Vulnerable Population if that employee uses PHEL for
a use inapplicable to an employee who is not a member of a Vulnerable Population. The
Company will not otherwise require the disclosure of health information for the use of
